# Cloudflare Pages / Netlify header rules.
#
# Pages' default for static assets is `public, max-age=14400` — four hours
# during which a browser serves its stored copy WITHOUT revalidating. None of
# the scripts below are content-hashed or version-queried, so a deploy stayed
# invisible in any tab that had already fetched them (this is exactly how a
# city-map.js fix shipped and could not be seen without a hard reload).
#
# index.html is already `max-age=0, must-revalidate` by default, so the page
# itself is always current; the rules here bring its local scripts and styles
# in line. Revalidation costs one conditional request each and returns a 304
# when nothing changed — negligible for four small files, and it means a
# deploy is visible immediately.

# NOTE: these rules are correct and ARE applied by Pages — confirmed against
# the origin, https://bailamos-web.pages.dev/site.css, which returns
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate`.
#
# The custom domain does NOT. www.getbailamos.app rewrites max-age to 14400
# (exactly four hours) on the way out, keeping `must-revalidate` from here but
# replacing the max-age. That is a ZONE setting, not something this file can
# reach: Caching -> Configuration -> Browser Cache TTL in the Cloudflare
# dashboard, which overrides origin Cache-Control for cacheable content types.
# Set it to "Respect Existing Headers" and these rules take effect.
#
# Tell-tale: .jsx files here DO get max-age=0 on the custom domain, because
# text/jsx is not a type the zone caches (cf-cache-status: DYNAMIC), so the
# override never touches them. .css and .js come back REVALIDATED and rewritten.
#
# Until the zone setting changes, a deploy that pairs an HTML change with a CSS
# change renders half-applied for up to four hours for returning visitors.

/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Images are the large files and change rarely — cache them hard. Any image
# that does change should be renamed rather than replaced in place.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# /go is the paid-campaign landing page. It is a real 200 HTML page, not a
# redirect — Meta's ad review crawler follows redirects and rejects an
# apps.apple.com destination (#1487810), so the hop it replaced could never
# pass review. Keep it out of search results; the page carries the same
# directive in a <meta name="robots"> tag, and this header covers fetchers
# that read headers only.
/go
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

/go.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
